      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks, that helps a lot. when i plug in the actual observed moment of inertia for the moon into that shell equation, the radius of the inner cavity $R_{inner}$ comes out almost identical to the outer radius $R_{outer}$, meaning the shell is incredibly thin relative to the total size. \n\nit actually supports the idea that it's an artificial construct rather than a planetary body. given the seismic data i mentioned earlier, if it's a hollowed-out structure, what kind of material science properties or structural reinforcement would be required to prevent such a thin shell from collapsing under its own gravitational load or tidal stresses from Earth? i'm trying to see if the \"lunar shell\" model is even physically viable under known material constraints.",
